================================================================================
Model weights and datasets — not covered by this file
================================================================================
KubeMetal ships no model weights and no datasets. The Model Hub downloads
whatever Hugging Face repository the user selects, and the Data tab ingests
whatever sources the user points it at. Those artifacts carry their own
licenses — frequently NOT OSI-approved (e.g. the Llama Community License, the
Qwen License, or CC-BY-NC dataset terms), and some restrict commercial use or
redistribution regardless of KubeMetal's own Apache-2.0 grant.

Checking the license of a downloaded model or dataset, and complying with it,
is the user's responsibility. KubeMetal's Apache-2.0 license covers the
application only.

Third-party Python packages (mlx-lm, mlx-vlm, lancedb, sentence-transformers,
dvc, prefect, lm-eval and their dependencies) are installed by the app into a
host venv from PyPI at the user's request; they are not redistributed here.
All of them are MIT or Apache-2.0 as of this writing.

================================================================================
Weak-copyleft components (MPL-2.0) — source availability
================================================================================
The KubeMetal binary links one Mozilla Public License 2.0 component:

  option-ext 0.2.0 — https://github.com/soc/option-ext
  (reached as a transitive dependency of `dirs`, used by `tauri` and `wry`)

MPL-2.0 §3.2 requires that recipients of an Executable Form be told how to
obtain the Source Code Form. KubeMetal uses this crate **unmodified**; its
complete corresponding source is published by its author at the URL above
and mirrored on crates.io at the exact version listed in that release's
`THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md`:

  https://crates.io/crates/option-ext/0.2.0

Four further MPL-2.0 crates appear in `Cargo.lock` and therefore in the SBOM
and notices — cssparser, cssparser-macros, dtoa-short, and selectors. These
are reached **only** through `tauri-utils`' optional `dom_query` dependency
on the `tauri-macros` proc-macro path, which is compiled for the build host
and is not linked into the shipped binary (verified with
`cargo tree -i <crate> --target aarch64-apple-darwin -e normal --no-dedupe`).
Their source is nonetheless available at https://github.com/servo/rust-cssparser,
https://github.com/servo/stylo, and https://github.com/upsuper/dtoa-short.

Beyond that one crate, nothing compiled into the KubeMetal binary is under
GPL, LGPL, AGPL, SSPL, BUSL, or any other license restricting commercial use.
(`r-efi`, whose license expression offers LGPL-2.1-or-later as one of three
alternatives, does not resolve into the macOS dependency graph at all; where
it does apply, the MIT alternative is selected.)

This statement is scoped to the binary. It says nothing about the container
images the user's cluster pulls at provision time — a Linux image layer
routinely contains GPL userland (busybox, coreutils, apk/apt), and the
upstream projects' own Apache-2.0 license covers only their code. KubeMetal
does not build, host, or redistribute those images: the manifests name them
and the user's cluster pulls them from their upstream registries.

One consequence worth stating plainly: the Air-Gap Management tab assembles a
bundle of those same images on the user's own machine (`docker pull` +
`docker save`). Carrying that bundle to one's own offline cluster is internal
use, but handing it to a third party is redistribution of the upstream images
and their GPL userland — with the source-offer obligations that implies. That
is the operator's call and the operator's obligation, not KubeMetal's.

